    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d688">

                <pb facs="0737" n="629" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg629"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.5112 France/76: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-12-30T15:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 30,
                                1927—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>227. Department’s circular of December 28.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d688-f1"/> This
                    morning I orally and confidentially informed British Ambassador of contents of
                    note to French Ambassador regarding Briand’s proposals. At the British
                    Ambassador’s suggestion, you are instructed to read to Sir Austen Chamberlain,
                    as strictly confidential, the text of the note to the French Ambassador. You
                    should not leave a copy of the text.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d688-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d688-f1" n="21" xml:id="d688-f1-content">
                <p>See footnote 18, <ref target="#pg626">p. 626</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d688-f2" n="22" xml:id="d688-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 1, Jan. 3, 1928, the Chargé in Great Britain reported that in the
                    absence of Sir Austen Chamberlain, British Secretary of State for Foreign
                    Affairs, he had read the note to Sir Victor Wellesley, Deputy Under Secretary of
                    State.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
